00:10:47.646 --> 00:10:48.695
We don't go inside.

00:10:48.695 --> 00:10:51.786
We get our groceries delivered
or we pick them up curbside.

00:10:51.786 --> 00:10:52.619
Yeah.

00:10:53.619 --> 00:10:55.317
It's just more of the same.

00:10:55.317 --> 00:10:58.074
And I no longer account.
Yeah.

00:10:58.074 --> 00:10:59.264
I mean, are you counting?

00:10:59.264 --> 00:11:01.899
Can you--
I did not count originally,

00:11:01.899 --> 00:11:04.439
so I wouldn't know where to start.

00:11:04.439 --> 00:11:07.460
Yeah, 'cause 50 days I was like 50 days,

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:09.678
what a huge deal.

00:11:09.678 --> 00:11:13.239
We were talking about this earlier,

00:11:13.239 --> 00:11:16.118
and I'm not a routine-oriented
person, right?

00:11:16.118 --> 00:11:18.870
I don't like to do the
same thing every day,

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:21.870
but something about us not traveling

00:11:23.688 --> 00:11:27.021
and not doing much besides shooting GMM,

00:11:29.473 --> 00:11:31.318
shooting this show,

00:11:31.318 --> 00:11:33.858
coming to the creative
house and doing the stuff

00:11:33.858 --> 00:11:38.369
that we're doing like on TikTok
or the social media stuff,

00:11:38.369 --> 00:11:42.536
it's made my mornings way
more predictable, right?

00:11:43.648 --> 00:11:45.568
And so I'm not going to the gym,

00:11:45.568 --> 00:11:48.252
I'm working out in my garage.

00:11:48.252 --> 00:11:50.252
I'm doing my back stuff.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:53.553
My exercises, my meditation,

00:11:54.681 --> 00:11:56.109
and I've never done those.

00:11:56.109 --> 00:11:58.651
Like, you get the little
alerts on your app

00:11:58.651 --> 00:12:00.010
that say that like--

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:01.040
It's time to meditate.

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:04.884
For 10 days in a row or 20 days in a row.

00:12:04.884 --> 00:12:07.255
I think I got to like 30
days in a row at one point.

00:12:07.255 --> 00:12:09.627
Skipped a day, had to start over,

00:12:09.627 --> 00:12:11.615
but I've never been that consistent

00:12:11.615 --> 00:12:12.787
with any of those things,

00:12:12.787 --> 00:12:15.370
and even though I'm stir-crazy,

00:12:16.955 --> 00:12:19.396
there's something about
getting into that rhythm

00:12:19.396 --> 00:12:22.544
of exercising and meditating
and actually having time

00:12:22.544 --> 00:12:24.834
for reflection and journaling, even,

00:12:24.834 --> 00:12:26.167
even journaling,

00:12:27.709 --> 00:12:29.445
I feel like I'm in a pretty good place.

00:12:29.445 --> 00:12:34.431
Yeah, and that makes me
angry at myself, not at you,

00:12:34.431 --> 00:12:37.003
because the opposite happened with me.

00:12:37.003 --> 00:12:38.913
Like right at the beginning,

00:12:38.913 --> 00:12:39.987
and I talked about this,

00:12:39.987 --> 00:12:43.373
I was like, I'm buckling down on routine,

00:12:43.373 --> 00:12:45.034
'cause I always love routine.

00:12:45.034 --> 00:12:47.455
And it's like, I now have
a opportunity to do more

00:12:47.455 --> 00:12:48.975
of the things I want to do

00:12:48.975 --> 00:12:50.886
incorporated into my routine every day.

00:12:50.886 --> 00:12:53.643
I can work out and
meditate on the same day,

00:12:53.643 --> 00:12:56.694
whereas before I would have to alternate

00:12:56.694 --> 00:12:58.350
or something like that.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:02.850
And I went, I think it was
50 days in a row meditating

00:13:04.062 --> 00:13:07.432
because the app kept up with that and--

00:13:07.432 --> 00:13:08.617
That's a lot.

00:13:08.617 --> 00:13:10.001
And then after that,

00:13:10.001 --> 00:13:11.641
it says your record was that,

00:13:11.641 --> 00:13:13.664
and now you've done two days in a row.

00:13:13.664 --> 00:13:15.304
Yeah, you gotta work back up to it.

00:13:15.304 --> 00:13:18.830
I've never done more than four
days in a row since I broke

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:20.351
the streak of 50.

00:13:20.351 --> 00:13:22.684
Oh.
And I was working out

00:13:23.953 --> 00:13:26.094
and I stopped doing that.

00:13:26.094 --> 00:13:27.182
I just got out of that.

00:13:27.182 --> 00:13:29.405
'Cause my shoulder started hurting again,

00:13:29.405 --> 00:13:31.238
and I got discouraged.

00:13:32.232 --> 00:13:35.639
And then it's like, oh, you
sleep a little bit later?

00:13:35.639 --> 00:13:38.889
And then I get down on myself about it.

00:13:40.762 --> 00:13:41.929
So I tried it,

00:13:43.368 --> 00:13:46.205
and then I was like, as long
as I just walk every day.

00:13:46.205 --> 00:13:48.643
You gotta keep doing something.

00:13:48.643 --> 00:13:49.476
Right.

00:13:49.476 --> 00:13:51.673
You gotta stay active.

00:13:51.673 --> 00:13:54.074
And so I just shifted from working out

00:13:54.074 --> 00:13:54.985
'cause of my shoulder

00:13:54.985 --> 00:13:57.231
to just walking and listening to a book.

00:13:57.231 --> 00:14:00.146
And then I got done with my book

00:14:00.146 --> 00:14:04.073
and it started getting
really hot really early.

00:14:04.073 --> 00:14:06.635
Yeah.

00:14:06.635 --> 00:14:08.494
And now I don't--
It's very high in this room.

00:14:08.494 --> 00:14:10.580
Can I just say that?
I like it.

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:12.439
I worked out this morning.

00:14:12.439 --> 00:14:16.232
So my internal temperature,
it's really high right now.

00:14:16.232 --> 00:14:17.570
Yes, so you look at me.

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:19.404
You shouldn't work out, man.
I'm sweating.

00:14:19.404 --> 00:14:22.020
You just wake up a little bit later.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:24.423
Don't feel as bad about yourself.

00:14:24.423 --> 00:14:26.913
I mean, there's like a flirtation

00:14:26.913 --> 00:14:28.782
with a form of depression here.

00:14:28.782 --> 00:14:32.143
I think it's 'cause
that's just the natural.

00:14:32.143 --> 00:14:33.833
I mean, when you're cooped up,

00:14:33.833 --> 00:14:35.461
that's not your way of life.

00:14:35.461 --> 00:14:36.352
It's difficult.

00:14:36.352 --> 00:14:38.601
And that's the kind of,

00:14:38.601 --> 00:14:40.613
I was talking about this in therapy.

00:14:40.613 --> 00:14:41.530
I was like,

00:14:45.936 --> 00:14:47.625
I do feel a little guilty, right?

00:14:47.625 --> 00:14:49.875
Because the average person,

00:14:51.186 --> 00:14:52.884
most people I know,

00:14:52.884 --> 00:14:54.976
are having a really difficult time.

00:14:54.976 --> 00:14:57.267
And I thought that, oh man,

00:14:57.267 --> 00:14:58.547
this is gonna be really difficult for me

00:14:58.547 --> 00:15:02.068
because I like things to be shaken up.

00:15:02.068 --> 00:15:03.063
I like to go places.

00:15:03.063 --> 00:15:03.896
I like to go out.

00:15:03.896 --> 00:15:05.406
I don't like eating at home.

00:15:05.406 --> 00:15:06.387
You know what I'm saying?

00:15:06.387 --> 00:15:07.696
But all I'm doing is eating at home.

00:15:07.696 --> 00:15:08.886
Yeah.

00:15:08.886 --> 00:15:12.017
But to me, it's a testament to the power

00:15:12.017 --> 00:15:13.623
of exercise and meditation.

00:15:13.623 --> 00:15:15.585
I dunno which one is more,

00:15:15.585 --> 00:15:20.002
but something about there
is this prophylactic effect

00:15:21.514 --> 00:15:24.846
of exercise on your mental wellbeing.

00:15:24.846 --> 00:15:26.954
And exercise and meditation.

00:15:26.954 --> 00:15:27.787
What?

00:15:27.787 --> 00:15:32.769
You mean, it's like a condom?
Like a condom for your mind.

00:15:32.769 --> 00:15:37.769
No, but what I'm saying is
that my experiences right now,

00:15:37.930 --> 00:15:39.947
the circumstances,

00:15:39.947 --> 00:15:41.508
and I'm not saying I haven't been down

00:15:41.508 --> 00:15:43.987
and I haven't had days of depression

00:15:43.987 --> 00:15:45.667
and sort of just like listlessness

00:15:45.667 --> 00:15:48.465
and just feeling like
I can't get motivated.

00:15:48.465 --> 00:15:49.854
I mean, there's been a couple of times

00:15:49.854 --> 00:15:51.134
we're working on a couple of things

00:15:51.134 --> 00:15:52.363
that require some writing.

00:15:52.363 --> 00:15:55.030
And some days it'll be like, oh,

00:15:57.235 --> 00:15:58.545
I could do this all day.

00:15:58.545 --> 00:15:59.518
And then some days it'll be like,

00:15:59.518 --> 00:16:02.195
I got nothing for this,

00:16:02.195 --> 00:16:03.924
but that's sort of typical anyway, right?

00:16:03.924 --> 00:16:06.016
You don't always have it.

00:16:06.016 --> 00:16:08.195
But I was talking to
my therapist and saying

00:16:08.195 --> 00:16:11.447
that there's just
something about I've never

00:16:11.447 --> 00:16:15.769
ever been this consistent
with my daily practice

00:16:15.769 --> 00:16:17.687
of exercise and meditation.

00:16:17.687 --> 00:16:20.398
And I'm experiencing the benefits of it.

00:16:20.398 --> 00:16:23.649
And like I said, I feel guilty,

00:16:23.649 --> 00:16:25.997
but also I'm reminded that

00:16:25.997 --> 00:16:27.796
when things do get back to normal,

00:16:27.796 --> 00:16:29.085
because they will,

00:16:29.085 --> 00:16:31.367
we will start going into the office.

00:16:31.367 --> 00:16:33.526
We'll start traveling again.

00:16:33.526 --> 00:16:34.728
We'll start going to things

00:16:34.728 --> 00:16:36.606
that kind of shift your schedule around,

00:16:36.606 --> 00:16:38.684
knock you out of your routine,

00:16:38.684 --> 00:16:41.042
getting home late from something.

00:16:41.042 --> 00:16:43.549
Getting home late, what is that like?

00:16:43.549 --> 00:16:45.008
I'm home every single night.

00:16:45.008 --> 00:16:47.960
I'm home already, you know?
Yeah.

00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:50.660
And so the idea of having

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:52.160
that routine shaken up.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:54.540
And walking too, I'm
doing a bunch of walking

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:57.382
and I didn't do any walking at all.

00:16:57.382 --> 00:16:58.850
Like I'm seeing parts of the neighborhood

00:16:58.850 --> 00:17:00.862
that I've never seen.

00:17:00.862 --> 00:17:04.629
And so I have this like desire to okay,

00:17:04.629 --> 00:17:06.130
how do you hold onto this?

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:08.478
Because I feel like if
I can take this practice

00:17:08.478 --> 00:17:10.739
into my normal busy life,

00:17:10.739 --> 00:17:12.562
when things get normal again,

00:17:12.562 --> 00:17:14.118
I'm gonna be better off.

00:17:14.118 --> 00:17:17.580
But I know I'm not gonna
be able to keep up with it.

00:17:17.580 --> 00:17:20.111
It's just the fact that I
have the freedom to do it

00:17:20.111 --> 00:17:22.973
is the only reason that I'm
being so consistent right now.

00:17:22.973 --> 00:17:27.723
But I mean, it's so hard to
process what you're saying.

00:17:30.793 --> 00:17:34.363
Like I said, it makes me
feel angry or just frustrated

00:17:34.363 --> 00:17:36.363
because I just feel like

00:17:39.235 --> 00:17:40.513
if you're not in that place,

00:17:40.513 --> 00:17:41.894
hearing somebody say they're in that place

00:17:41.894 --> 00:17:44.589
doesn't encourage you to be in that place.

00:17:44.589 --> 00:17:47.506
At least for me, it's like, "Well."

00:17:52.655 --> 00:17:54.632
I think if I started working out

00:17:54.632 --> 00:17:56.291
and doing something more consistently,

00:17:56.291 --> 00:17:57.890
I think that would help me.

00:17:57.890 --> 00:17:59.393
But maybe I just need
to say, "You know what?"

00:17:59.393 --> 00:18:03.846
"It's okay to just not do
that for a little while."

00:18:03.846 --> 00:18:04.679
And it is okay.

00:18:04.679 --> 00:18:07.029
Because I have to be ready

00:18:07.029 --> 00:18:09.529
to launch back into something.

00:18:10.967 --> 00:18:15.490
But I do think I feel the
cumulative problems associated

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:17.121
with not doing that.

00:18:17.121 --> 00:18:18.050
You know?
Right?

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:21.763
Because I do think it
spills into you get so antsy

00:18:21.763 --> 00:18:24.180
with everything else that
we are not able to do

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:26.099
in the way we want to do it.

00:18:26.099 --> 00:18:26.932
Yeah.

00:18:26.932 --> 00:18:28.488
You know?

00:18:28.488 --> 00:18:30.363
And that's the weird thing.

00:18:30.363 --> 00:18:33.048
The fact that we're not
working on something

00:18:33.048 --> 00:18:37.465
that is taking us out of
the office and into meetings

00:18:38.571 --> 00:18:40.231
and you know what I'm saying?

00:18:40.231 --> 00:18:42.379
Like that's--
People interaction.

00:18:42.379 --> 00:18:46.341
That's like what I live
for is the dynamic nature

00:18:46.341 --> 00:18:48.110
of our jobs, right?

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:51.769
I mean, we have a lot that is
the same over and over again,

00:18:51.769 --> 00:18:53.762
but we have a lot that
makes it very dynamic

00:18:53.762 --> 00:18:55.620
and it makes it interesting
and you're collaborating,

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:56.750
and you're trying new things,

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:58.131
and most of the things people never see,

00:18:58.131 --> 00:19:00.168
but we're working on things.

00:19:00.168 --> 00:19:03.871
And that's why I can only
go back to, "Well, what's?"

00:19:03.871 --> 00:19:06.661
"Oh, it's because I'm
exercising every single day."

00:19:06.661 --> 00:19:10.603
It's like, I'm not having
time to let those thoughts

00:19:10.603 --> 00:19:11.688
and those frustrations,

00:19:11.688 --> 00:19:14.359
which under any other circumstances

00:19:14.359 --> 00:19:18.609
would make me feel down
and make me feel depressed.

00:19:19.841 --> 00:19:22.190
So yeah, to me, it's just like,
okay, I gotta remember this.

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:23.479
I just gotta remember it.

00:19:23.479 --> 00:19:25.931
Yeah, I don't know.

00:19:25.931 --> 00:19:27.475
I'm just trying to be better.
I'm sorry if I

00:19:27.475 --> 00:19:28.728
made you feel worse.

00:19:28.728 --> 00:19:29.978
But I'm just saying--

00:19:29.978 --> 00:19:31.656
Hey, you're speaking your truth.

00:19:31.656 --> 00:19:33.607
It's separate from me.

00:19:33.607 --> 00:19:36.507
And I'm all I'm saying is that as someone

00:19:36.507 --> 00:19:40.424
who has thankfully not
stopped these processes,

00:19:42.927 --> 00:19:46.269
I think that's the only thing
keeping me together right now.

00:19:46.269 --> 00:19:49.138
'Cause it's definitely
not in most circumstances.

00:19:49.138 --> 00:19:51.220
Yeah.
Right?

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:53.137
And again, it's like,

00:19:54.153 --> 00:19:55.422
we don't have nearly as many worries

00:19:55.422 --> 00:19:56.772
as a lot of people.

00:19:56.772 --> 00:19:59.479
Oh, yeah, right.
You know?

00:19:59.479 --> 00:20:01.073
So let's move on.

00:20:01.073 --> 00:20:04.563
We're gonna talk about kisses.

00:20:04.563 --> 00:20:06.143
First kisses.

00:20:06.143 --> 00:20:09.873
But first we're gonna
talk about mythical.com.

00:20:09.873 --> 00:20:12.552
It's a website we started a while back.

00:20:12.552 --> 00:20:13.832
Lots of good stuff.

00:20:13.832 --> 00:20:16.935
There's always something to
surprise you at mythical.com.

00:20:16.935 --> 00:20:19.685
The good mythical summer T-shirt.

00:20:20.975 --> 00:20:22.543
Check that out.

00:20:22.543 --> 00:20:25.498
This thing will make you happy.

00:20:25.498 --> 00:20:28.122
Sometimes it's just about
putting on the right thing,

00:20:28.122 --> 00:20:30.382
deciding to get fully dressed,

00:20:30.382 --> 00:20:33.572
and just seem ready for life.

00:20:33.572 --> 00:20:36.205
Like, brush your teeth,
put on some clothes.

00:20:36.205 --> 00:20:37.972
And if you put on the right shirt,

00:20:37.972 --> 00:20:39.885
even though you may not
be experiencing the summer

00:20:39.885 --> 00:20:41.631
that you're used to,

00:20:41.631 --> 00:20:44.473
the good mythical summer T-shirt

00:20:44.473 --> 00:20:47.504
might be that little extra
thing that you needed.

00:20:47.504 --> 00:20:50.193
We're not doing it in
tank-top this year, right?

00:20:50.193 --> 00:20:52.342
The tank option?

00:20:52.342 --> 00:20:53.175
I don't know.

00:20:53.175 --> 00:20:54.354
Because I could use a tank-top right now.

00:20:54.354 --> 00:20:57.326
I'm feeling like--
There's some tank options

00:20:57.326 --> 00:20:59.231
that are gonna be happy--
It is so hot

00:20:59.231 --> 00:21:00.623
under my arms right now.

00:21:00.623 --> 00:21:02.640
In the mythical store.

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:03.932
There's tank options?

00:21:03.932 --> 00:21:06.262
Well, I think that's in the works, yeah,

00:21:06.262 --> 00:21:07.885
but I don't know if it's of this shirt.

00:21:07.885 --> 00:21:09.525
I can't remember right now.
Well I request one of those.

00:21:09.525 --> 00:21:12.064
You got some water-skiing
fish-head people.

00:21:12.064 --> 00:21:14.195
I mean, that is just fun.
Yeah.

00:21:14.195 --> 00:21:15.933
Mythical.com.
You know what?

00:21:15.933 --> 00:21:17.045
I feel better now.

00:21:17.045 --> 00:21:18.245
I feel totally better!

00:21:18.245 --> 00:21:19.584
You thought about selling T-shirts

00:21:19.584 --> 00:21:22.645
and that made you feel better?
Yeah.

00:21:22.645 --> 00:21:24.795
Before we talk about kisses,

00:21:24.795 --> 00:21:27.265
we didn't talk about what's on your nose.

00:21:27.265 --> 00:21:28.552
Is it noticeable?

00:21:28.552 --> 00:21:31.654
I don't know if you can see it on camera,

00:21:31.654 --> 00:21:33.326
but I mean,

00:21:33.326 --> 00:21:34.594
I asked you about it and you were like,

00:21:34.594 --> 00:21:35.865
I'll tell you on the podcast,

00:21:35.865 --> 00:21:39.013
so I'm gonna have to ask.
Okay, is it a scab?

00:21:39.013 --> 00:21:40.134
It's a scratch.

00:21:40.134 --> 00:21:44.967
'Cause when I got up this
morning it was a bloody thing.

00:21:47.262 --> 00:21:49.061
The end of my nose just had a bloody scab.

00:21:49.061 --> 00:21:50.470
It looks like it just happened.

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:51.762
Oh, really?
It looks like you just got

00:21:51.762 --> 00:21:52.963
scratched by a bobcat.

00:21:52.963 --> 00:21:55.713
Last night...
Was it a bobcat?

00:21:57.021 --> 00:21:59.110
No, it was a tree.

00:21:59.110 --> 00:21:59.943
Oh.

00:21:59.943 --> 00:22:02.341
I was throwing some Frisbee with my boys.

00:22:02.341 --> 00:22:05.241
We like to go out and throw
some Frisbee, you know?

00:22:05.241 --> 00:22:08.152
In the street?
In the street, yeah.

00:22:08.152 --> 00:22:12.032
And Lincoln threw the
Frisbee up in the tree

00:22:12.032 --> 00:22:15.994
and it was just high
enough where if I jumped,

00:22:15.994 --> 00:22:18.183
I think I could grab the Frisbee.

00:22:18.183 --> 00:22:22.412
'Cause I shook the limbs in the lemon tree

00:22:22.412 --> 00:22:23.682
and it didn't fall.

00:22:23.682 --> 00:22:24.782
And that's not a euphemism,

00:22:24.782 --> 00:22:25.883
you actually have a lemon tree?

00:22:25.883 --> 00:22:26.716
Right,

00:22:26.716 --> 00:22:31.241
and so then I was like, I
can jump and I can grab it.

00:22:31.241 --> 00:22:32.593
But what I didn't take into account

00:22:32.593 --> 00:22:37.011
was in between where I
would jump and grab it,

00:22:37.011 --> 00:22:39.103
there were a bunch of other limbs.

00:22:39.103 --> 00:22:39.936
Yeah.

00:22:39.936 --> 00:22:40.991
So when I jumped up--

00:22:40.991 --> 00:22:42.154
The lower limbs, they call it.

00:22:42.154 --> 00:22:44.441
The lower limbs that were right there.

00:22:44.441 --> 00:22:46.743
You know when you're
focusing on something,

00:22:46.743 --> 00:22:48.746
things closer to you get out of focus.

00:22:48.746 --> 00:22:50.394
Just let this be a lesson.

00:22:50.394 --> 00:22:54.522
So I raised my hand and it
was like Superman taking off,

00:22:54.522 --> 00:22:57.801
like fist in the air,

00:22:57.801 --> 00:22:59.209
except that all of these limbs

00:22:59.209 --> 00:23:01.778
that were apparently
just inches from my face

00:23:01.778 --> 00:23:04.043
before I started to jump--

00:23:04.043 --> 00:23:05.261
Were now in your face?

00:23:05.261 --> 00:23:09.433
Were just, like, I
jumped right into limbs.

00:23:09.433 --> 00:23:12.344
Oh, it could've been worse.

00:23:12.344 --> 00:23:13.677
I mean, thank God I wear glasses.

00:23:13.677 --> 00:23:15.260
Right.
You know?

00:23:16.808 --> 00:23:21.808
Even if I had 20/20
vision without glasses,

00:23:21.860 --> 00:23:23.300
it turns out I would
need to wear them just

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:25.518
for like, eye protection.
Kurt Rambis.

00:23:25.518 --> 00:23:26.513
Yeah.

00:23:26.513 --> 00:23:29.881
I mean, I could have lost
my eyesight last night.

00:23:30.906 --> 00:23:33.482
But instead it hit my glasses
and it hit the end of my nose.

00:23:33.482 --> 00:23:35.163
And I'm like, man, that really hurt.

00:23:35.163 --> 00:23:36.157
And when I went inside,

00:23:36.157 --> 00:23:37.775
there was like a big bloody spot there

00:23:37.775 --> 00:23:39.608
on the end of my nose.

00:23:41.094 --> 00:23:45.511
There's a life lesson here.

00:23:46.531 --> 00:23:48.833
Don't get so focused on the Frisbee

00:23:48.833 --> 00:23:50.449
that you lose sight of the limbs

00:23:50.449 --> 00:23:51.729
right in front of your face.

00:23:51.729 --> 00:23:53.991
I think the life lesson is use a broom,

00:23:53.991 --> 00:23:55.278
get a broom.

00:23:55.278 --> 00:23:56.220
Yeah, that's a--

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:58.973
Why jump when you can get a broom?

00:23:58.973 --> 00:24:02.763
Interesting that we
had similar situations.

00:24:02.763 --> 00:24:07.346
This morning I had to grab
a suitcase for my wife,

00:24:07.346 --> 00:24:09.800
which we keep stacked up

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:13.003
on this shelf in the garage.
Okay.

00:24:13.003 --> 00:24:16.503
Because she's taking a little beach trip

00:24:18.198 --> 00:24:19.031
with the kids.

00:24:19.031 --> 00:24:20.715
A sabbatical from you.

00:24:20.715 --> 00:24:24.081
And then I later will join them.

00:24:24.081 --> 00:24:24.914
But

00:24:28.824 --> 00:24:29.846
so it's up high,

00:24:29.846 --> 00:24:32.374
and I was like, "I can't reach that."

00:24:32.374 --> 00:24:35.964
I could potentially jump to grab it.

00:24:35.964 --> 00:24:38.483
But I'm 42.
There's limbs in your face?

00:24:38.483 --> 00:24:41.243
I'm 42, I don't jump for things.

00:24:41.243 --> 00:24:43.435
So many things could go wrong.

00:24:43.435 --> 00:24:47.174
So I grabbed a golf club
because that was the thing

00:24:47.174 --> 00:24:48.233
that was closest to me,

00:24:48.233 --> 00:24:50.355
but I couldn't get either side,

00:24:50.355 --> 00:24:52.273
neither the grip nor the club head

00:24:52.273 --> 00:24:54.287
to get under the handle--
Underneath the handle.

00:24:54.287 --> 00:24:58.496
Because the handle, it was
one of those Away suitcases.

00:24:58.496 --> 00:25:01.043
And so they're like, flush
until you pull on them.

00:25:01.043 --> 00:25:02.364
Do you got a step ladder?

00:25:02.364 --> 00:25:03.954
Yeah, but that was on the other side

00:25:03.954 --> 00:25:05.273
of the garage.
Of the garage?

00:25:05.273 --> 00:25:06.273
Oh goodness.

00:25:07.414 --> 00:25:09.511
I'm 42, you can't walk across your garage?

00:25:09.511 --> 00:25:11.724
Mr. Still Working Out
Everyday can't walk over

00:25:11.724 --> 00:25:12.652
to a step-ladder.

00:25:12.652 --> 00:25:13.983
No, no, I can.

00:25:13.983 --> 00:25:16.102
It's just, I don't know if I should.

00:25:16.102 --> 00:25:16.935
Okay?

00:25:16.935 --> 00:25:18.874
But then I was like, shoot, man.

00:25:18.874 --> 00:25:20.212
I actually did think to myself,

00:25:20.212 --> 00:25:21.144
"You've been working out."

00:25:21.144 --> 00:25:23.564
"You can jump, you can jump up there."

00:25:25.593 --> 00:25:29.375
And thankfully there were
no lower limbs in the way.

00:25:29.375 --> 00:25:31.212
There was nothing but air
between me and the suitcase.

00:25:31.212 --> 00:25:32.141
You jumped?

00:25:32.141 --> 00:25:33.130
I jumped,

00:25:33.130 --> 00:25:38.130
and it was like, first try
slipped my fingers right under

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:42.037
the handle and beckoned
the suitcase my way.

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:44.600
Then when I came back down,

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:46.171
I did the smart thing,

00:25:46.171 --> 00:25:48.992
I released as I was coming back down,

00:25:48.992 --> 00:25:52.313
so as to leave it dislodged
but not falling on me.

00:25:52.313 --> 00:25:53.462
Uh-huh.

00:25:53.462 --> 00:25:56.719
And then I was able to do a baby jump,

00:25:56.719 --> 00:25:57.920
grab it and pull it down.

00:25:57.920 --> 00:25:59.343
Did it hit you in the face?

00:25:59.343 --> 00:26:01.367
No, no, no, no facial injuries.

00:26:01.367 --> 00:26:03.303
No life lesson to be learned, I guess.

00:26:03.303 --> 00:26:06.177
No, I just got the suitcase.

00:26:06.177 --> 00:26:11.069
You know, I think what you
described is not unlike

00:26:11.069 --> 00:26:12.192
a first kiss.

00:26:12.192 --> 00:26:15.332
You've gotta really work up your gumption.

00:26:15.332 --> 00:26:16.900
You gotta convince yourself

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:19.323
that you can go for it.
You gotta think about it.

00:26:19.323 --> 00:26:21.932
And then you gotta reach up,

00:26:21.932 --> 00:26:25.515
and you gotta have a
gentle sort of caress.

00:26:26.832 --> 00:26:29.090
Well, you should be gentle.

00:26:29.090 --> 00:26:30.942
Based on some of these stories,

00:26:30.942 --> 00:26:32.461
that is not always the case.
The moment where you

00:26:32.461 --> 00:26:35.513
can really latch on is
when you should let go,

00:26:35.513 --> 00:26:37.583
and then go for a second.

00:26:37.583 --> 00:26:39.902
Delicate jump.
You talking about a tease?

00:26:39.902 --> 00:26:44.319
No, just, there's plenty
of time for a second kiss.

00:26:46.244 --> 00:26:50.140
The first kiss should not
try to be the end-all be-all.

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:52.452
It's virtually impossible.

00:26:52.452 --> 00:26:54.619
Now we've done an episode,

00:26:55.491 --> 00:26:57.908
I believe it was episode 114,

00:26:58.780 --> 00:27:00.402
about first kiss stories.

00:27:00.402 --> 00:27:01.235
Uh-huh.

00:27:01.235 --> 00:27:02.831
And when we decided to do
this episode, we were like,

00:27:02.831 --> 00:27:04.491
"Oh, it's our first episode back.

00:27:04.491 --> 00:27:05.792
"First, first, first.

00:27:05.792 --> 00:27:07.420
"First kisses, let's do that!"

00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:10.049
And then we didn't even
realize we had done an episode.

00:27:10.049 --> 00:27:11.382
Right.

00:27:11.382 --> 00:27:13.123
This is not an uncommon issue with us.

00:27:13.123 --> 00:27:14.632
Until Kiko pointed it out.

00:27:14.632 --> 00:27:15.532
Right.

00:27:15.532 --> 00:27:16.762
But I think subconsciously we're looking

00:27:16.762 --> 00:27:18.562
for any chance to talk,

00:27:18.562 --> 00:27:22.219
and our wives support this assertion,

00:27:22.219 --> 00:27:24.900
to talk about our first kisses

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:26.299
or our first girlfriends

00:27:26.299 --> 00:27:28.230
or our second girlfriends.

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:31.538
You know, we always go back to them.

00:27:31.538 --> 00:27:35.791
But in that episode we did
share our first kiss stories.

00:27:35.791 --> 00:27:38.310
I think we can revisit them a little bit.

00:27:38.310 --> 00:27:39.231
If you haven't heard that,

00:27:39.231 --> 00:27:40.293
we'll give you the gist of it

00:27:40.293 --> 00:27:42.281
and maybe have a different take on it.

00:27:42.281 --> 00:27:43.491
If you're really interested,

00:27:43.491 --> 00:27:44.722
you can go back and listen to that.

00:27:44.722 --> 00:27:47.940
We had a couple of stories
from listeners in there.

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:49.739
And then we had a couple
of crew members come on

00:27:49.739 --> 00:27:50.860
and tell their stories,

00:27:50.860 --> 00:27:54.277
but we got some good stuff from you guys.

00:27:57.171 --> 00:27:58.004
Yeah.

00:27:58.004 --> 00:28:02.603
So we'll keep our recounting
our stories relatively short

00:28:02.603 --> 00:28:04.032
since we've told them before.

00:28:04.032 --> 00:28:06.496
And we've actually told them
outside of the podcast as well.

00:28:06.496 --> 00:28:08.862
But the update on my story is

00:28:08.862 --> 00:28:13.195
that you actually had the
privilege of being with me

00:28:14.202 --> 00:28:18.617
when we visited the
location of my first kiss

00:28:18.617 --> 00:28:20.449
during our trip back to Buies Creek,

00:28:20.449 --> 00:28:24.172
and I invited you to reenact it with me.

00:28:24.172 --> 00:28:25.815
And then we showed that on LTAT.

00:28:25.815 --> 00:28:27.399
You made me hold your hand.

00:28:27.399 --> 00:28:29.599
And I mean, there was no kissing.

00:28:29.599 --> 00:28:30.432
Right.

00:28:30.432 --> 00:28:31.265
That's true.

00:28:31.265 --> 00:28:32.098
I didn't go for it.

00:28:32.098 --> 00:28:34.429
But I wasn't comfortable
with getting close to that.

00:28:34.429 --> 00:28:36.240
Yeah, I could tell.

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:38.719
It was at the--
It was at the fountain.

00:28:38.719 --> 00:28:41.987
So that video's on an episode of LTAT

00:28:41.987 --> 00:28:46.268
that aired after our Buies
Creek documentary episode

00:28:46.268 --> 00:28:48.346
from I guess last year.

00:28:48.346 --> 00:28:50.026
But I'm gonna give you a little background

00:28:50.026 --> 00:28:52.427
I'm not sure I've given before.

00:28:52.427 --> 00:28:55.954
And of course I had begun dating,

00:28:55.954 --> 00:28:59.060
or going with is what we
called it at the time, Amber,

00:28:59.060 --> 00:29:02.946
and I knew that tonight was the night.

00:29:02.946 --> 00:29:04.368
I don't know how long we'd been going out,

00:29:04.368 --> 00:29:06.554
but it was like you
start going with somebody

00:29:06.554 --> 00:29:07.938
and then you start kissing 'em;

00:29:07.938 --> 00:29:09.305
this is the order of things.

00:29:09.305 --> 00:29:11.808
Well, the order of things
is that I date Amber

00:29:11.808 --> 00:29:13.289
and kiss her first.

00:29:13.289 --> 00:29:15.221
She's my first kiss.
Right.

00:29:15.221 --> 00:29:17.371
And then you date her after me

00:29:17.371 --> 00:29:19.149
and she becomes your first kiss.

00:29:19.149 --> 00:29:20.827
Did you actually date her?

00:29:20.827 --> 00:29:21.801
Yeah.

00:29:21.801 --> 00:29:22.708
For how long?

00:29:22.708 --> 00:29:25.162
But you just kissed her that time

00:29:25.162 --> 00:29:27.227
and then probably didn't kiss her again?

00:29:27.227 --> 00:29:30.310
Well, okay, so I--
Right or wrong?

00:29:31.170 --> 00:29:34.023
I can't imagine that you kissed her again.

00:29:34.023 --> 00:29:34.856
I don't--

00:29:34.856 --> 00:29:36.330
That would be very surprising to me.

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:37.349
I don't remember.

00:29:37.349 --> 00:29:39.369
'Cause that was seventh grade.

00:29:39.369 --> 00:29:41.786
Yeah, it was seventh grade.

00:29:44.329 --> 00:29:46.251
I don't think I kissed her again.

00:29:46.251 --> 00:29:49.641
I did.

00:29:49.641 --> 00:29:51.497
Do you want to tell your story first?

00:29:51.497 --> 00:29:54.267
It was at a birthday party at

00:29:54.267 --> 00:29:57.266
the Lillington Ruritan building

00:29:57.266 --> 00:30:00.338
and there was like a big open dance floor.

00:30:00.338 --> 00:30:02.576
Nobody was dancing.
What is a Ruritan?

00:30:02.576 --> 00:30:03.577
I dunno.

00:30:03.577 --> 00:30:06.638
It's people who ruritate.
Is it like they broke off

00:30:06.638 --> 00:30:08.913
from the Puritans?

00:30:08.913 --> 00:30:11.528
So like, "Yeah, we're not
quite as pure as the Puritans."

00:30:11.528 --> 00:30:13.606
Or maybe they ruined the Puritans.

00:30:13.606 --> 00:30:15.458
Yeah, I think they decimated the Puritans.

00:30:15.458 --> 00:30:16.718
They decimated the Puritans?

00:30:16.718 --> 00:30:18.767
They tainted the Puritans.

00:30:18.767 --> 00:30:21.907
We rurinted them.

00:30:21.907 --> 00:30:23.556
So it's like a place of debauchery,

00:30:23.556 --> 00:30:25.943
which, interesting, is
why you kissed there.

00:30:25.943 --> 00:30:29.027
We were sitting beside each
other shoulder-to-shoulder

00:30:29.027 --> 00:30:30.785
in these like folding chairs

00:30:30.785 --> 00:30:33.219
that were like on the
edge of a dance floor

00:30:33.219 --> 00:30:35.779
that nobody was dancing on and--

00:30:35.779 --> 00:30:37.358
The best.
Michael and Anna

00:30:37.358 --> 00:30:38.707
were sitting next to us,

00:30:38.707 --> 00:30:41.874
and basically they sent me the message

00:30:43.592 --> 00:30:45.540
that I was supposed to kiss Amber.

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:47.705
This was the time, you know?

00:30:47.705 --> 00:30:48.956
Amber wanted me to kiss her,

00:30:48.956 --> 00:30:51.057
but like, we didn't
communicate about this.

00:30:51.057 --> 00:30:54.259
It was just Michael looking
over at me and saying,

00:30:54.259 --> 00:30:55.926
"Go for her."
Yeah.

00:30:57.785 --> 00:30:59.486
Yeah, so I went in for the kiss,

00:30:59.486 --> 00:31:02.486
and the story goes that he timed it.

00:31:04.214 --> 00:31:05.853
I can't remember how many seconds it was.

00:31:05.853 --> 00:31:08.156
I can't remember what I said
how many seconds it was.

00:31:08.156 --> 00:31:11.693
You have to go back and
listen to the three years ago

00:31:11.693 --> 00:31:13.543
when I told this story.

00:31:13.543 --> 00:31:15.506
But Michael timed it?

00:31:15.506 --> 00:31:17.084
We knew.
Yeah.

00:31:17.084 --> 00:31:20.693
And I mean, it was multiple seconds.

00:31:20.693 --> 00:31:25.213
Like, I'm gonna say
either seven, nine or 14.

00:31:25.213 --> 00:31:26.624
I don't know what I said before.

00:31:26.624 --> 00:31:30.692
And I was more reliable
in telling the story then

00:31:30.692 --> 00:31:31.953
than I am now,

00:31:31.953 --> 00:31:34.365
'cause I haven't thought about
it probably much since then.

00:31:34.365 --> 00:31:35.662
We need to connect with Michael,

00:31:35.662 --> 00:31:38.662
because Michael plays a pivotal role

00:31:41.630 --> 00:31:43.058
in my story as well.

00:31:43.058 --> 00:31:46.808
Because I mean, not only
was he there with...

00:31:49.226 --> 00:31:51.505
It wouldn't have been
with Anna in eighth grade.

00:31:51.505 --> 00:31:53.467
That would have been you
or it would have been?

00:31:53.467 --> 00:31:54.747
That was me, yeah.

00:31:54.747 --> 00:31:56.644
He was with Anna in seventh grade.

00:31:56.644 --> 00:31:58.523
Who was he with that night?

00:31:58.523 --> 00:31:59.467
Anna.

00:31:59.467 --> 00:32:01.642
No, who was he with at eighth grade?

00:32:01.642 --> 00:32:02.475
My night.

00:32:03.375 --> 00:32:04.875
Who was he with?

00:32:06.623 --> 00:32:07.456
I don't know.

00:32:07.456 --> 00:32:08.623
He was always with somebody though.

00:32:08.623 --> 00:32:09.910
He was always with somebody.

00:32:09.910 --> 00:32:11.282
And he had confidence.

00:32:11.282 --> 00:32:16.083
The thing for me when I revisit
the story was that I did it

00:32:16.083 --> 00:32:18.205
because I felt like I should do it.

00:32:18.205 --> 00:32:23.205
Like, I didn't have a
sex drive at the time.

00:32:24.544 --> 00:32:25.394
I didn't.

00:32:25.394 --> 00:32:27.296
I mean, I was a late bloomer.
Yes.

00:32:27.296 --> 00:32:28.629
It came later.

00:32:29.975 --> 00:32:30.982
I guess no pun intended.

00:32:32.764 --> 00:32:34.721
But it was like, "I gotta do this."

00:32:34.721 --> 00:32:36.368
You felt pressure, you felt pressure.

00:32:36.368 --> 00:32:38.156
Yeah, I mean, it was literally like,

00:32:38.156 --> 00:32:39.686
you need to make out.

00:32:39.686 --> 00:32:40.953
You felt external pressure.

00:32:40.953 --> 00:32:43.705
I felt internal pressure, like a geyser.

00:32:43.705 --> 00:32:45.313
Like a literal Geyser.

00:32:45.313 --> 00:32:47.284
And it was a long kiss.

00:32:47.284 --> 00:32:48.765
I remember she was on my right.

00:32:48.765 --> 00:32:52.715
So I had to go over and my
head tilted to the left.

00:32:52.715 --> 00:32:55.028
And I mean, it was a French kiss.

00:32:55.028 --> 00:32:56.528
It was like, okay.

00:32:58.415 --> 00:33:01.536
As I've said, many times, I was like,

00:33:01.536 --> 00:33:03.068
I gotta be ready for this.

00:33:03.068 --> 00:33:06.275
I had practiced on my bed post.

00:33:06.275 --> 00:33:07.645
Right.

00:33:07.645 --> 00:33:09.996
It might be a little
odd that that same bed

00:33:09.996 --> 00:33:12.188
is still in our house
and Lando sleeps in it,

00:33:12.188 --> 00:33:13.296
but it is painted.

00:33:13.296 --> 00:33:14.205
Have you told him that?

00:33:14.205 --> 00:33:15.038
No.

00:33:15.038 --> 00:33:16.196
Have you showed him which post it was?

00:33:16.196 --> 00:33:17.611
Son, come here, let me show you something.

00:33:17.611 --> 00:33:19.867
It was a round post.

00:33:19.867 --> 00:33:21.268
A very face-like post.

00:33:21.268 --> 00:33:22.641
But it was like a head.

00:33:22.641 --> 00:33:24.769
It was like making out
with a shrunken head.

00:33:24.769 --> 00:33:26.191
Yeah, her head is at least four times

00:33:26.191 --> 00:33:28.200
the size of that bedpost.

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:29.807
So that was probably
difficult to navigate.

00:33:29.807 --> 00:33:32.474
And it was just a wood veneer.

00:33:33.939 --> 00:33:36.449
It was a stained wood.
Yes, it had no mouth.

00:33:36.449 --> 00:33:38.161
But it wasn't white paint like it is now.

00:33:38.161 --> 00:33:39.920
It's not nearly as kissable.

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:42.503
And also the wall of my shower.

00:33:43.522 --> 00:33:46.287
